In ASP.NET Core, you can password protect your website on the front-end using the built-in authentication and authorization features. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you set up basic authentication using the `web.config` file:

1. First, ensure that you have the `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.Basic` package installed in your project. You can install it using the NuGet Package Manager or the Package Manager Console:

   ```
   Install-Package Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.Basic
   ```

2. In your `Startup.cs` file, add the following code to the `ConfigureServices` method to register the basic authentication service:

   ```csharp
   services.AddAuthentication(BasicAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
       .AddBasicAuthentication(options =>
       {
           options.Realm = "YourRealmName";
           options.Events = new BasicAuthenticationEvents
           {
               OnValidatePrincipal = context =>
               {
                   // Validate the username and password here
                   if (context.Username == "your_username" && context.Password == "your_password")
                   {
                       var claims = new List<Claim>
                       {
                           new Claim(ClaimTypes.Name, context.Username)
                       };

                       var identity = new ClaimsIdentity(claims, BasicAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme);
                       var principal = new ClaimsPrincipal(identity);

                       context.Principal = principal;
                       context.Success();
                   }

                   return Task.CompletedTask;
               }
           };
       });
   ```

3. In the `Configure` method, add the following code to enable authentication and authorization:

   ```csharp
   app.UseAuthentication();
   app.UseAuthorization();
   ```

4. Now, you can protect your controllers or actions using the `[Authorize]` attribute:

   ```csharp
   [Authorize]
   public class HomeController : Controller
   {
       // Your actions here
   }
   ```

5. To configure the `web.config` file for IIS, add the following code inside the `<system.webServer>` section:

   ```xml
   <security>
       <authentication>
           <basicAuthentication enabled="true" realm="YourRealmName" />
       </authentication>
   </security>
   ```

This setup will enable basic authentication for your ASP.NET Core website using the `web.config` file. However, please note that basic authentication sends the username and password in plain text, so it's not recommended for production environments. Consider using more secure authentication methods like OAuth, OpenID Connect, or ASP.NET Core Identity for better security.
</Text>

